.TH GIT-STONE 1 "" "git-stone" "Git Stone Manual"
.SH NAME
git-stone \- save task boundaries as you work, turn them into atomic commits later
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBgit stone\fR <name> [\fB\-d\fR <text>] [\fB\-\-allow\-empty\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR]
\fBgit stone \-\-\fR <name>
\fBgit stone\fR <name> \fB\-x\fR <pattern> [\fB\-\-remember\fR]
\fBgit stone guard\fR [\fBblock\fR|\fBwarn\fR|\fBoff\fR]
\fBgit stone exclude\fR [\fB\-\-remove\fR <pattern> | \fB\-\-clear\fR]
\fBgit stone\fR [\fBstatus\fR] [\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
\fBgit stone list\fR [\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
\fBgit stone review\fR
\fBgit stone diff\fR <id|current> [<git diff args>]
\fBgit stone show\fR <id>
\fBgit stone\fR <id> \fBcommit\fR [\fB\-m\fR <msg> | \fB\-F\fR <file> | \fB\-e\fR | \fB\-\-no\-verify\fR | \fB\-S\fR | \fB\-\-author\fR | \fB\-\-date\fR | \fB\-s\fR]
\fBgit stone commit\-all\fR [\fB\-\-edit\fR]
\fBgit stone\fR <id> \fBdrop\fR
\fBgit stone drop all\fR
\fBgit stone repair\fR [\fB\-\-apply\fR]
\fBgit stone adopt\fR <branch|detached>
\fBgit stone prune\fR [\fB\-\-apply\fR] [\fB\-\-chain\fR <ref>]
\fBgit stone agents\fR
\fBgit stone setup \-\-agent\fR <claude|codex|both> [\fB\-\-global\fR]
\fBgit stone setup \-\-update\fR
\fBgit stone help\fR | \fBversion\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \fIstone\fR is a snapshot of the whole working tree, saved when a task ends. It is stored as a Git tree under
\fIrefs/stones/\fR. A stone's diff starts at the previous stone, so it shows only that task's changes. Stones can
later be committed one by one with \fBgit stone <id> commit\fR, which makes an ordinary \fBgit commit\fR whose
content is exactly that stone. Making a stone never changes the branch, the working tree or the index.
.PP
A stone is a boundary, not a branch. Dropping a stone removes the boundary, not the code. Stones are sequential
and may depend on earlier stones. Final commits are ordinary Git commits.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBgit stone\fR <name> [\fB\-d\fR <text>]
Make a stone. \fB\-d\fR stores a longer description of what the task was and why.
\fB\-\-allow\-empty\fR allows a stone with no changes. \fB\-\-force\fR allows a stone on a tree that equals the
branch commit (usually after a stash). Use \fB\-\-\fR before a name that looks like a command, a number or starts with \-.
.TP
\fBgit stone\fR <name> \fB\-x\fR <pattern> [\fB\-\-remember\fR]
Leave paths out of this stone. A pattern is a Git pathspec, so \fI*.log\fR and \fIscratch/\fR both work; quote it
so the shell does not expand it. A tracked file that matches keeps its version from the previous stone (or the branch
commit), so it counts as unchanged. An untracked file that matches is not in the stone. \fB\-x\fR can be repeated.
\fB\-\-remember\fR also saves the patterns as \fIstone.exclude\fR in this repository's own Git config, so every
later stone leaves them out too, on every branch. The rules are never pushed or cloned. Each stone prints what was left out.
.TP
\fBguard\fR [\fBblock\fR|\fBwarn\fR|\fBoff\fR]
While a branch has stones, refuse (\fBblock\fR) or warn about (\fBwarn\fR) plain git commands that move it, so that
agents must use \fBgit stone <id> commit\fR. It installs a \fIreference\-transaction\fR hook, which Git also runs for
\fBcommit \-\-no\-verify\fR, and saves \fIstone.guard\fR in the repository's git config. Git Stone's own commits, new stones,
tags and branches without stones are always allowed. A hook of your own, or a \fIcore.hooksPath\fR folder, is never touched; the
three lines to add are printed instead. Without an argument it shows the current setting.
.TP
\fBexclude\fR [\fB\-\-remove\fR <pattern> | \fB\-\-clear\fR]
Show the remembered patterns, remove one, or remove all. The next stone then includes those files again.
.TP
\fBstatus\fR, \fBlist\fR
Show the stones and the current changes, or only the stones. \fB\-\-porcelain\fR prints version\-tagged,
tab\-separated, NUL\-terminated records for scripts.
.TP
\fBreview\fR
For every stone: description, files changed and line counts; then the current changes.
.TP
\fBdiff\fR <id|current>, \fBshow\fR <id>
Show a stone's diff (extra arguments go to \fBgit diff\fR), or the unstoned changes, or one stone in full.
.TP
<id> \fBcommit\fR
Commit the first stone. Runs a real \fBgit commit\fR, so hooks, signing and the editor work.
.TP
\fBcommit\-all\fR [\fB\-\-edit\fR]
Commit every stone in order, using the stone names as messages.
.TP
<id> \fBdrop\fR, \fBdrop all\fR
Remove a boundary, or every boundary of this branch. No files change. Works also when the branch moved, so a chain that \fBrepair\fR cannot replay can be cleared.
.TP
\fBrepair\fR [\fB\-\-apply\fR]
After the branch moved, replay the stones onto the new HEAD with Git's three\-way merge. Without \fB\-\-apply\fR it
only checks and prints a plan. Needs Git 2.40 or newer.
.TP
\fBadopt\fR <branch|detached>
Move a chain to the branch you are on, after a rename or after \fBgit switch \-c\fR from a detached HEAD.
.TP
\fBprune\fR [\fB\-\-apply\fR] [\fB\-\-chain\fR <ref>]
List, or with \fB\-\-apply\fR remove, empty chains of deleted branches and removed worktrees and stale temporary
files. A chain that still holds stones is removed only when named with \fB\-\-chain\fR.
.TP
\fBagents\fR
Print a block to paste into an AI agent's memory file.
.TP
\fBsetup \-\-agent\fR <claude|codex|both> [\fB\-\-global\fR]
Add the same block to the agent's memory file (\fICLAUDE.md\fR or \fIAGENTS.md\fR) in this project, or with
\fB\-\-global\fR in the agent's own folder. Existing text is kept; the block sits between marker lines and is
refreshed in place when run again.
.TP
\fBsetup \-\-update\fR
Refresh the block in every memory file that already has one: \fICLAUDE.md\fR and \fIAGENTS.md\fR of this project, and
the agents' own files in the home folder. Creates nothing. \fBstatus\fR warns when a block is older than this version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 1 refused, with a message that says why and nothing changed; 2 internal or Git failure.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fIrefs/stones/heads/<branch>\fR
The stones of a branch.
.TP
\fIrefs/stones/detached/main\fR, \fIrefs/stones/detached/linked/<name>\fR
The stones made on a detached HEAD, per worktree.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBGIT_STONE_FAILPOINT\fR
Test hook: makes the process exit with status 99 at a named point.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgit\fR(1). Full documentation: https://github.com/HarshKothari88/git-stone
